{"event":"changes_requested","job_id":"docs-payment-methods-apple-pay-api-only-apple-pay-token-decryption","reason":"Independently read all 813 lines of the pinned raw and verified SHA-256, five exact quotes, candidate and suggestion consistency, concept route, and Q1/Q2. The PCI-gated decryption branches, merchant certificate/key and replay checks, conditional CIT ECI, MIT table-versus-example distinction, masked PAN caveat, and payment-outcome boundary are preserved. One central in-app handoff claim misidentifies the property path: the source's Swift callback accesses `payment.token.paymentData` (raw lines 43-46), but the candidate and Q1 call it `PKPayment.paymentData`. The raw prose loosely says PKPayment includes paymentData, so the entry should quote the actual nested access rather than imply a direct property on PKPayment. A reader implementing the stated path could select the wrong value."}
{"event":"changes_requested","job_id":"docs-payment-methods-cards-click-to-pay","reason":"Independently read all 391 lines of the pinned raw and verified SHA-256, receipt/candidate/suggestions identity, five exact quote locations, and the root-to-Adyen-index-to-online-payments route. The candidate preserves Mastercard-only recurring scope, Web/Checkout versions, Customer Area and Support gates, OTP/400 qualification, malformed request snippets, scheme sandbox testing, and the payment-outcome boundary. One central flow distinction remains unclear: the Advanced requirements table lists API-only with a Custom Card Component (raw lines 41-49), but the standalone Click to Pay Component instructions explicitly start with creating a Session and pass it to AdyenCheckout (raw lines 161-174). The candidate and Q1 list the API-only path without this Sessions condition, so a reader choosing a standalone Click to Pay integration could select the wrong server flow."}
